From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Petr=20=C5=A0tetiar?= <petr.stetiar@prplfoundation.org>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mac80211_hwsim: add self-managed testbed regtest mode
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

hwsim's existing custom-world regtest modes (regtest=5/6/10, the
hwsim_world_regdom_custom_0{1..4} tables) apply their regdomain with
REGULATORY_CUSTOM_REG + wiphy_apply_custom_regulatory(). That is not
enough to keep a fixed regdomain on a hwsim radio: reg_get_regdomain()
in net/wireless/reg.c only prefers a wiphy's own regd over the global
one when the last regulatory request was not USER- or
COUNTRY_IE-initiated, and ignore_reg_update() only skips CORE-initiated
updates for REGULATORY_CUSTOM_REG wiphys. So a plain USER hint (e.g.
hostapd or a management layer pushing "US" via
NL80211_CMD_REQ_SET_REG) still makes the wiphy fall back to the global
regdb. On an x86-64 QEMU testbed running Wi-Fi 7 tests with regtest=5
and a USER hint of US, this silently re-applies the US regdb to every
hwsim wiphy, which flags all 6 GHz channels NO_IR, and the 5 GHz rules
in custom_03 are 40 MHz only, well short of what 802.11be (EHT) needs.

Add HWSIM_REGTEST_TESTBED (regtest=11) as a new mode built for exactly
this: an automated testbed that needs a regdomain immune to whatever
hint an AP stack pushes at runtime, with 5 GHz channels up to 160 MHz
and 6 GHz channels up to 320 MHz available for EHT link tests.

The new hwsim_world_regdom_testbed regdomain (alpha2 "US") covers:
 - 2.4 GHz, 2402-2482 MHz, 40 MHz max, same as the existing tables;
 - 5 GHz, 5170-5925 MHz, 160 MHz max, no DFS, no NO_IR;
 - 6 GHz, 5945-7125 MHz, 320 MHz max, no NO_IR (this part is
   numerically identical to custom_03's 6 GHz rule, which was never
   the problem - the problem was custom_03's wiphy still listening to
   USER hints).
hwsim has no real RF front end, so there is nothing to CAC/DFS for and
nothing that needs NO_IR before it may transmit; each band is one wide
rule at the target width rather than several DFS/non-DFS sub-band
rules with NL80211_RRF_AUTO_BW (that flag lets cfg80211 derive a wider
channel across adjacent narrower-declared rules, e.g. custom_04's
UNII-1/2A/2C split - not needed here since nothing splits the band).
The 5 GHz and 6 GHz rules deliberately touch at 5935 MHz rather than
overlap, so hwsim's whole 5 GHz channel table up to 5925 MHz (chan
185) stays covered.

Report alpha2 "US" to match country_code=US in the QEMU testbed's
pWHM-generated hostapd configuration. With "99", hostapd reads a
mismatching country through GET_REG with NL80211_ATTR_WIPHY and arms
its five-second COUNTRY_UPDATE timeout on every interface (re)add.
This extends the same-radio outage during VAP changes, so tests
sample sibling APs before they return to Enabled. Matching "US" skips
that wait; hostapd still sends its normal country hint. A configuration
using another country can still incur the wait.

This is a testbed identity, not the wireless-regdb US rule set. Keep the
three permissive rules unchanged: self-management, not alpha2 "99",
protects them from core and user hints. Installing this per-wiphy table
does not set the global regdomain; userspace hints may change the global
domain independently.

To make the mode immune to later hints, the radio is registered as
REGULATORY_WIPHY_SELF_MANAGED instead of REGULATORY_CUSTOM_REG:
ignore_reg_update() returns true unconditionally for a SELF_MANAGED
wiphy, regardless of the hint's initiator. REGULATORY_WIPHY_SELF_MANAGED
cannot be combined with REGULATORY_CUSTOM_REG (core.c, wiphy_register()
WARN_ON()s and rejects that combination), so this mode skips
wiphy_apply_custom_regulatory() entirely; the new
hwsim_new_radio_params.selfmanaged_reg flag picks between the two at
radio-creation time and leaves every existing regtest mode's
CUSTOM_REG path untouched.

For a SELF_MANAGED wiphy, the regdomain is installed after
registration with regulatory_set_wiphy_regd_sync(), which per its own
kerneldoc "requires the RTNL and the wiphy mutex to be held" and calls
ASSERT_RTNL(). mac80211_hwsim_new_radio() does not hold either lock at
that point: ieee80211_register_hw() just above it calls
wiphy_register(), which itself does rtnl_lock()/rtnl_unlock() around
its own body, and that could not succeed if the caller already held
RTNL. So both locks are taken explicitly around the sync call,
mirroring the same rtnl_lock()+wiphy_lock() pairing
ath12k_regd_update() (drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/reg.c) uses when
it calls regulatory_set_wiphy_regd_sync() outside of an RTNL context.
The sync variant is used instead of the async
regulatory_set_wiphy_regd() so the regdomain and channel flags are
final before mac80211_hwsim_new_radio() returns and before any
netlink/hostapd/iw caller can observe the new radio - there is no
"give the workqueue a chance to run" sleep to reason about, unlike the
existing CUSTOM_REG path just above it.

param->reg_alpha2 / regulatory_hint() is intentionally left unset for
this mode: a SELF_MANAGED wiphy also ignores country IE and beacon
hints (wiphy_regulatory_register() ORs in
REGULATORY_DISABLE_BEACON_HINTS | REGULATORY_COUNTRY_IE_IGNORE for
such wiphys), so a driver-hint alpha2 would be redundant with the
regdomain already installed above.

The hwsim_world_regdom_testbed table is intentionally not added to
hwsim_world_regdom_custom[]: that array also backs
HWSIM_ATTR_REG_CUSTOM_REG on the HWSIM_CMD_NEW_RADIO netlink command,
which only ever applies a regdomain the CUSTOM_REG way. Reusing the
same table there would make it just as overridable by a later USER
hint as custom_03 already is, defeating the point.

The original mode, before the alpha2 revision, was compile-checked
out-of-tree: this patch was applied on top of a backports-6.12.61 tree
already built and linked against a prplOS
x86_64 6.6.119 kernel configuration, and drivers/net/wireless/virtual/
mac80211_hwsim.c was recompiled with the exact recorded cflags
(-Werror, -std=gnu11, the kernel's regular defconfig) using
x86_64-openwrt-linux-musl-gcc from that build's toolchain. It compiled
without warnings or errors, and mac80211_hwsim.o's symbol table shows
the new hwsim_world_regdom_testbed rodata plus undefined references to
regulatory_set_wiphy_regd_sync()/rtnl_lock()/rtnl_unlock() as
expected. It was not linked into a full backports-6.12.96 module or
booted; see patches/README.md for DUT-side validation steps.

Signed-off-by: Petr Štetiar <petr.stetiar@prplfoundation.org>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/virtual/mac80211_hwsim.c | 102 +++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 98 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/virtual/mac80211_hwsim.c b/drivers/net/wireless/virtual/mac80211_hwsim.c
index a260c39989cb..47d1d5f628d7 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/virtual/mac80211_hwsim.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/virtual/mac80211_hwsim.c
@@ -120,6 +120,15 @@ MODULE_PARM_DESC(multi_radio, "Support Multiple Radios per wiphy");
  * 	    domain request
  * 	6 and on - should follow the intersection of the 3rd, 4rth and 5th radio
  * 	           regulatory requests.
+ * @HWSIM_REGTEST_TESTBED: Used for automated testbeds that need wide,
+ *	DFS-free 5 GHz (up to 160 MHz) and 6 GHz (up to 320 MHz) channels
+ *	that stay available regardless of any USER/COUNTRY_IE regulatory
+ *	hint an AP stack (e.g. hostapd) pushes at runtime. Unlike
+ *	%HWSIM_REGTEST_CUSTOM_WORLD, the radio is registered as
+ *	%REGULATORY_WIPHY_SELF_MANAGED, so cfg80211 core and user hints are
+ *	ignored for it (see ignore_reg_update() in net/wireless/reg.c) and
+ *	the regdomain below is applied directly with
+ *	regulatory_set_wiphy_regd_sync().
  *
  * These are the different values you can use for the regtest
  * module parameter. This is useful to help test world roaming
@@ -142,6 +151,7 @@ enum hwsim_regtest {
 	HWSIM_REGTEST_STRICT_ALL = 8,
 	HWSIM_REGTEST_STRICT_AND_DRIVER_REG = 9,
 	HWSIM_REGTEST_ALL = 10,
+	HWSIM_REGTEST_TESTBED = 11,
 };
 
 /* Set to one of the HWSIM_REGTEST_* values above */
@@ -218,6 +228,39 @@ static const struct ieee80211_regdomain *hwsim_world_regdom_custom[] = {
 	&hwsim_world_regdom_custom_04,
 };
 
+/*
+ * Self-managed regdomain for HWSIM_REGTEST_TESTBED. It is deliberately not
+ * part of hwsim_world_regdom_custom[]: that array is also reachable from
+ * userspace via HWSIM_ATTR_REG_CUSTOM_REG on HWSIM_CMD_NEW_RADIO, which only
+ * ever applies a regdomain the CUSTOM_REG way (wiphy_apply_custom_regulatory()).
+ * This regdomain must only ever be applied through the self-managed path in
+ * mac80211_hwsim_new_radio(), or it would be just as overridable by a later
+ * USER/COUNTRY_IE regulatory hint as hwsim_world_regdom_custom_03 already is.
+ *
+ * There is no DFS and no NO_IR here: hwsim has no real RF front-end, so
+ * there is nothing to detect radar on and nothing that needs a channel
+ * availability check before it may transmit. The 5 GHz and 6 GHz rules are
+ * each a single wide rule capped at the widest channel width mac80211_hwsim
+ * advertises (160 MHz / 320 MHz) rather than a set of sub-band rules with
+ * NL80211_RRF_AUTO_BW: that flag exists to let cfg80211 derive a wider
+ * channel across adjacent rules that individually declare a narrower
+ * max_bandwidth (e.g. custom_04's DFS/non-DFS UNII sub-bands); it is not
+ * needed when one rule already spans the whole band at the target width.
+ * The 5 GHz upper edge (5935 MHz margin) and the 6 GHz lower edge (also
+ * 5935) intentionally touch rather than overlap, so every hwsim 5 GHz
+ * channel up to 5925 MHz (chan 185) stays covered without abutting the
+ * 6 GHz rule.
+ */
+static const struct ieee80211_regdomain hwsim_world_regdom_testbed = {
+	.n_reg_rules = 3,
+	.alpha2 = "US",
+	.reg_rules = {
+		REG_RULE(2402 - 10, 2482 + 10, 40, 0, 20, 0),
+		REG_RULE(5170 - 10, 5925 + 10, 160, 0, 30, 0),
+		REG_RULE(5945 - 10, 7125 + 10, 320, 0, 33, 0),
+	}
+};
+
 struct hwsim_vif_priv {
 	u32 magic;
 	u32 skip_beacons[IEEE80211_MLD_MAX_NUM_LINKS];
@@ -4031,6 +4074,7 @@ struct hwsim_new_radio_params {
 	const char *reg_alpha2;
 	const struct ieee80211_regdomain *regd;
 	bool reg_strict;
+	bool selfmanaged_reg;
 	bool p2p_device;
 	bool use_chanctx;
 	bool multi_radio;
@@ -5500,10 +5544,24 @@ static int mac80211_hwsim_new_radio(struct genl_info *info,
 		hw->wiphy->regulatory_flags |= REGULATORY_STRICT_REG;
 	if (param->regd) {
 		data->regd = param->regd;
-		hw->wiphy->regulatory_flags |= REGULATORY_CUSTOM_REG;
-		wiphy_apply_custom_regulatory(hw->wiphy, param->regd);
-		/* give the regulatory workqueue a chance to run */
-		schedule_timeout_interruptible(1);
+		if (param->selfmanaged_reg) {
+			/*
+			 * REGULATORY_WIPHY_SELF_MANAGED may not be combined
+			 * with REGULATORY_CUSTOM_REG (core.c, wiphy_register()
+			 * rejects that combination), and unlike CUSTOM_REG the
+			 * regdomain itself is not installed here: it is
+			 * applied below with regulatory_set_wiphy_regd_sync()
+			 * once the wiphy is registered, which is the only
+			 * place that call is valid.
+			 */
+			hw->wiphy->regulatory_flags |=
+				REGULATORY_WIPHY_SELF_MANAGED;
+		} else {
+			hw->wiphy->regulatory_flags |= REGULATORY_CUSTOM_REG;
+			wiphy_apply_custom_regulatory(hw->wiphy, param->regd);
+			/* give the regulatory workqueue a chance to run */
+			schedule_timeout_interruptible(1);
+		}
 	}
 
 	wiphy_ext_feature_set(hw->wiphy,
@@ -5531,6 +5589,38 @@ static int mac80211_hwsim_new_radio(struct genl_info *info,
 
 	wiphy_dbg(hw->wiphy, "hwaddr %pM registered\n", hw->wiphy->perm_addr);
 
+	if (param->regd && param->selfmanaged_reg) {
+		int reg_err;
+
+		/*
+		 * hwsim_new_radio_nl() (netlink) and the regtest module
+		 * param loop in mac80211_hwsim_init() both call this
+		 * function without RTNL or the wiphy lock held: proven by
+		 * ieee80211_register_hw() -> wiphy_register() above, which
+		 * takes and releases rtnl_lock() itself and would deadlock
+		 * here otherwise. regulatory_set_wiphy_regd_sync() requires
+		 * RTNL (ASSERT_RTNL() in net/wireless/reg.c), so take it
+		 * explicitly, plus the wiphy lock, mirroring how e.g.
+		 * ath12k_regd_update() (drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/reg.c)
+		 * calls the same function from outside an RTNL context.
+		 * The sync variant is used, not the async
+		 * regulatory_set_wiphy_regd(), so the regdomain and channel
+		 * flags are final by the time this function returns and
+		 * before any userspace tool (hostapd, iw) can observe the
+		 * new radio.
+		 */
+		rtnl_lock();
+		wiphy_lock(hw->wiphy);
+		reg_err = regulatory_set_wiphy_regd_sync(
+			hw->wiphy, (struct ieee80211_regdomain *)param->regd);
+		wiphy_unlock(hw->wiphy);
+		rtnl_unlock();
+		if (reg_err)
+			wiphy_err(hw->wiphy,
+				  "mac80211_hwsim: regulatory_set_wiphy_regd_sync failed: %d\n",
+				  reg_err);
+	}
+
 	if (param->reg_alpha2) {
 		data->alpha2[0] = param->reg_alpha2[0];
 		data->alpha2[1] = param->reg_alpha2[1];
@@ -6880,6 +6970,10 @@ static int __init init_mac80211_hwsim(void)
 				break;
 			}
 			break;
+		case HWSIM_REGTEST_TESTBED:
+			param.regd = &hwsim_world_regdom_testbed;
+			param.selfmanaged_reg = true;
+			break;
 		default:
 			break;
 		}
